<Site title="AI & Governance | Bench2Bytes"><div class="page-heading"><p class="eyebrow">AI &amp; GOVERNANCE</p><h1>Responsible innovation.<br />Grounded in evidence.</h1><p class="lede">A scientific perspective on AI, data stewardship and trustworthy research, built across experimental biology, genomics, clinical data and real-world evidence.</p></div>
<section class="section prose"><p class="eyebrow">01 / OPERATIONAL EXPERIENCE</p><h2>Stewardship in practice</h2><p>My research work includes data-use agreements, institutional access coordination, IRB and HIPAA requirements, vendor/data-use considerations, quality controls, SOPs and reproducible computational infrastructure. These practices connect permitted use, provenance and scientific reliability.</p><p>In oncology data strategy, fit-for-purpose assessment begins with the clinical question. In omics and genomic surveillance, documented environments, pipelines and quality checks support reproducibility. Accountability depends on clear responsibilities and traceable decisions.</p></section>
<section class="section prose"><p class="eyebrow">02 / TECHNICAL &amp; TRANSFERABLE EXPERIENCE</p><h2>Validation before interpretation</h2><p>Omics pipelines, statistical modeling and biological interpretation provide a foundation for evaluating AI-enabled research. My current oncology work also connects scientific objectives, data readiness and analytical priorities.</p><p>My responsible-use perspective asks whether the data represent the intended population, whether evaluation matches the intended use, what limitations affect generalizability, and where human review is needed. Bias, explainability, provenance, privacy and reliability belong in that discussion throughout the lifecycle.</p><a class="text-link" href={url('projects.html#cell-therapy')}>Explore the single-cell &amp; multi-omics work →</a></section>
<section class="section prose"><p class="eyebrow">03 / DEVELOPING PROFESSIONAL DIRECTION</p><h2>From research stewardship to broader AI governance</h2><p>Enterprise AI governance, formal AI policy and institutional risk frameworks are developing professional interests. I am interested in bringing a scientist’s understanding of context, validation and evidence into these broader conversations.</p><p>The practical question remains consistent: what evidence supports this use, who is accountable, and what review is needed as the system or its context changes?</p></section>
<section class="section prose"><p class="eyebrow">BENCH2BYTES / KNOWLEDGE RESOURCE</p><h2><a href={url("governance-observatory.html")}>AI Governance Observatory</a></h2><p>A curated, source-tracked view of policy and guidance shaping biomedical AI, clinical research and genomic data.</p></section><section class="section prose"><h2>Scientific perspective, shared</h2><p><a href={url('blog-2026-10-05-AIGovernance.html')}>AI governance in biomedical research</a> examines evidence and institutional oversight.</p><p><a href={url('blog-2025-01-09-ClinicalTrials.html')}>Historical writing on clinical trials, AI and omics</a> explores bias, explainability and scientific interpretation.</p><a class="text-link" href={url('research-models.html')}>Explore the professional operating model →</a></section></Site>
